    <section columns="2" column-gap="l">
      <layout margin="40pt">
        <column gap="m">
          <text font-size="l" font="Helvetica-Bold" color="primary">A note in the second column</text>
          <text align="justify">The lamp-keepers of the northern coast kept a record of every evening they lit the harbour light, and the record grew longer than the harbour itself. Each entry gives the hour, the weather and the ships that passed before midnight. Most nights the third column stayed empty, and the keepers took pride in that. The oldest keeper claimed that she could tell the season from the smell of the oil alone, and she was seldom wrong. Nobody could say when the first entry was made, only that the ink in it had faded to the color of weak tea.</text>
          <text align="justify">Under the lamp room stood a cupboard of spare wicks, glass chimneys and one brass bell that nobody remembered buying. A page of the record was ruled into three columns: the hour, the weather, and a line for anything worth remembering. Visitors who read the record in order noticed that the handwriting changed every few years, while the weather never did. In the winter of the great storm the record runs to four pages for a single night. The oil came by boat in spring and autumn, and a late boat meant a careful winter.</text>
          <text align="justify">A keeper's daughter wrote the entries for six years when her father's hands failed, and her hand is the clearest in the book. The tower was painted every third summer, white below and red at the gallery. Visitors were few, and the book of their names fills less than a single page. The ones who came by sea signed with the name of their boat; the ones who came by the coast road signed with the name of their village.</text>
          <text align="justify">When the board sent the new lens, it came in eleven crates and a letter of instructions longer than the record of that year. The keepers set it up in a week and lit it on a calm night, and the entry for that night says only that the light could be seen from the church at Ardmore. The old lamp was kept in the cupboard under the lamp room, beside the brass bell.</text>
          <text align="justify">It was lit once more, on the night the new light failed in a gale, and the entry for that night is the longest in the book: three pages in a hand that grows smaller as it goes. The record ends when the light was made automatic. The last entry gives the hour, the weather and the ships that passed, as the first one did, and below it a line in the daughter's hand: kept to the end.</text>
          <text align="justify">The book is in the museum at the old customs house, in forty volumes, and the third column of the last page is empty. Readers who come to the museum ask most often about the night of the gale, and the keeper who wrote three pages in a shrinking hand.</text>
          <text align="justify">The museum keeps the old lamp beside the book, and lights it once a year, on the night of the gale, for as long as the oil in it lasts. The museum also keeps the letter of instructions that came with the new lens, and the reply the keepers sent. The reply is one line: received, and lit<fn>The reply is framed in the long room, beside the letter it answers.</fn>.</text>
        </column>
